Q: how to use as.Date on multiple columns now with dplyr? A: <p>The literal <em>fix</em> for your mistake is because <code>across</code> needs two args, a <em>selector</em> and a <em>function</em> (or rlang-style tilde expression). In your code, you're only passing the first. The literal fix is:</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>eq |&gt;
  mutate(
    across(survey_date, ~ as.Date(.x, &quot;%m/%d/%y&quot;))
  )
</code></pre>
<p>But really, you don't need <code>across</code> when it's only one variable, it's more direct to do just</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>eq |&gt;
  mutate(survey_date = as.Date(survey_date, &quot;%m/%d/%y&quot;))
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>The canonical discussion of <code>across()</code> is at <a href="https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/articles/colwise.html?q=across#why-do-we-like-across" rel="nofollow noreferrer">https://dplyr.tidyverse.org/articles/colwise.html?q=across#why-do-we-like-across</a>, where they demonstrate it in a myriad of ways:</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>df |&gt;
  group_by(g1, g2) |&gt;
  summarise(
    across(where(is.numeric), mean),
    across(where(is.factor), nlevels),
    n = n(),
  )
starwars |&gt;
  group_by(species) |&gt;
  filter(n() &gt; 1) |&gt;
  summarise(across(c(sex, gender, homeworld), n_distinct))
min_max &lt;- list(
  min = ~min(.x, na.rm = TRUE),
  max = ~max(.x, na.rm = TRUE)
)
starwars |&gt; summarise(across(where(is.numeric), min_max))
</code></pre>
<p>They have a &quot;translation&quot; section citing</p>
<pre class="lang-r prettyprint-override"><code>df |&gt; mutate_if(is.numeric, ~mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE))
# -&gt;
df |&gt; mutate(across(where(is.numeric), ~mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE)))

df |&gt; mutate_at(vars(c(x, starts_with(&quot;y&quot;))), mean)
# -&gt;
df |&gt; mutate(across(c(x, starts_with(&quot;y&quot;)), mean))

df |&gt; mutate_all(mean)
# -&gt;
df |&gt; mutate(across(everything(), mean))
</code></pre>
